<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Kampong Crate - Product Media</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="media-section">
      <h2>Kampong Crate Product Showcase</h2>

      <div class="content-wrapper">
        <!-- Step 1 & 2: Embedded video with controls, poster, and fallback message -->
        <div class="video-container">
          <video controls poster="box.png" width="420">
            <source src="Htoo.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            <!-- Fallback message for unsupported browsers -->
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
            <a href="Htoo.mp4">Download the video here</a>.
          </video>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 3: Inline SVG icon next to the video -->
        <div class="icon-container">
          <svg
            class="styled-svg"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="64"
            height="64"
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          >
            <path
              d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5zM2 17l10 5 10-5M2 12l10 5 10-5"
              fill="none"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
            />
          </svg>
          <span class="icon-label">Quality Assured</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 4: Discussion & Justification Note -->
      <section class="discussion-note">
        <h3>Step 4: SVG vs. Canvas Discussion Note</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Would this SVG icon be better as a canvas instead?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>No, SVG is a much better choice for this icon. Here is why:</em>
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Scalability & Crispness:</strong> SVG is vector-based,
            meaning it scales infinitely to any screen resolution (Retina
            displays, mobile, desktop) without losing sharpness or becoming
            pixelated. Canvas is raster-based and can look blurry when scaled.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CSS Styling:</strong> SVGs allow direct styling via CSS
            (such as changing `fill` or `stroke` colors dynamically on hover or
            theme changes), whereas a Canvas requires complex JavaScript pixel
            manipulation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accessibility & SEO:</strong> SVGs are text-based XML
            elements, making them indexable by search engines and readable by
            screen readers for accessibility. Canvas contents are invisible to
            screen readers.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
